# Dear to Me, production settings. Copy this file to deploy/.env on the server and fill it in:
#   cp .env.example .env && nano .env
# deploy/.env holds secrets: never commit it (git ignores it), never paste it anywhere.
# REQUIRED marks what production needs. Everything else may stay empty.

# ---------------- the site ----------------

# REQUIRED. The domain the site answers on, without https:// (Caddy gets its certificate for it).
# The app's D2M_ORIGIN becomes https://<this> (docker-compose.yml sets it).
D2M_DOMAIN=deartome.fi

# REQUIRED. An email Let's Encrypt may write to about the certificate.
ACME_EMAIL=

# How many proxies stand in front of the app: 1 = Caddy (DNS only, the grey cloud in Cloudflare);
# 2 = Cloudflare's proxy and Caddy (the orange cloud; also uncomment trusted_proxies in the Caddyfile).
D2M_TRUST_PROXY=1

# Set by docker-compose.yml, not here: NODE_ENV=production, D2M_DATA=/data, PORT=5174,
# D2M_ORIGIN=https://${D2M_DOMAIN}.
# NEVER set D2M_DEMO in production (the demo people sign in with a published password; the server
# refuses to start with it).

# ---------------- payments: Stripe ----------------
# Without STRIPE_SECRET_KEY payments are off and everything is free (fine for a closed test, not a launch).

# REQUIRED for payments. Stripe Dashboard > Developers > API keys > Secret key (sk_live_... for real
# money, sk_test_... to try it first). A restricted key is not enough (Checkout, Connect, refunds).
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# REQUIRED with payments. Stripe Dashboard > Developers > Webhooks > your endpoint > Signing secret
# (whsec_...). See README step 7. Without it the webhook is off: a buyer who closes the tab before
# coming back is charged but not granted, and subscriptions ended at Stripe stay on here.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ---------------- email: Cloudflare Email Service ----------------
# Email verification, password reset links and email change notices. See README step 13.
# Without these, no email is sent: the server only logs to whom and what (sign-up still works, but
# nobody can confirm their address or reset a password). NEVER set D2M_MAIL=log in production (the
# server refuses to start with it).

# cloudflare (send through Cloudflare Email Service) or log (send nothing). Empty: cloudflare when
# the two settings below are filled in. With cloudflare, the server refuses to start if one of them
# or MAIL_FROM is missing.
MAIL_PROVIDER=
# Cloudflare dashboard > Account home > Search (Ctrl+K) > "Copy account ID"
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=
# An API token with the permission Email Sending: Edit (an account-owned token is recommended:
# Manage Account > API Tokens). Not the R2 keys: a token of its own.
CLOUDFLARE_EMAIL_TOKEN=
# The sender, on the domain onboarded in Email Service > Email Sending (README step 13).
MAIL_FROM=Dear to Me <noreply@deartome.fi>
# Where replies go (optional; empty: replies go to MAIL_FROM, which nobody reads).
MAIL_REPLY_TO=

# ---------------- a closed beta ----------------
# 1: a slim banner on every page says the service is in test use, and how payments stand (off; Stripe
# test mode with the test card 4242 4242 4242 4242; or live, with no test card hint). Empty: no beta.
D2M_BETA=
# With D2M_BETA=1: the invite codes sign-up asks for, comma-separated (any one works, many times; the
# account keeps the code it used). Empty: open sign-up. Ignored without D2M_BETA=1.
D2M_INVITE_CODES=
# The usernames that moderate, comma-separated (an @ before one is fine): they see the moderation queue
# at /mod (reported posts, complaints, suspensions, the log) and make the decisions. List only accounts
# that exist; a moderator cannot change their username. Empty: no one moderates (reports still hide a
# post from For You after three, but no one can decide on them).
D2M_MODERATORS=

# ---------------- media: Cloudflare R2 ----------------
# Without these, pictures and videos stay on the server's disk (in the d2m-data volume), and vlogs
# go through the server. With them, media lives in the bucket (private, signed addresses).

# The bucket's S3 address: https://<account id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<bucket>
R2_URL=
# Cloudflare > R2 > Manage R2 API Tokens: Object Read & Write, for this bucket
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=

# More addresses the browser may upload into the bucket from (comma-separated); the site's own
# (https://${D2M_DOMAIN}) is always allowed by `npm run r2:cors`. Usually empty, or https://www.<domain>.
D2M_ORIGINS=

# ---------------- backups off the server: R2 ----------------
# Where `npm run backup:r2` copies the newest database backup: a bucket of its own (recommended),
# optionally with a prefix: https://<account id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com/<backup bucket>[/<prefix>]
D2M_BACKUP_URL=
# A key that may write the backup bucket (none: R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID and R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY are used,
# which works only if that key covers the backup bucket too)
D2M_BACKUP_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
D2M_BACKUP_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
